tually, the key point for the plan is:\n1. Reproduce Meta's GISMo Hit@1 = 20.56 using the official code.\n2. Reproduce Rita's 34.53 using their protocol.\n3. Isolate the difference (data split, candidate set, preprocessing).\n4. Then run width sweep 64-512 (hidden/emb_d) only inside whichever protocol reproduces, and report width trend under both.\n\nWait, re-reading the option summary: \"Then run the width sweep 64–512 only inside whichever protocol reproduces, and report the width trend under both.\"\n\nActually the summary says: \"First reproduce both numbers in one harness: Meta's official GISMo code against the Rita et al. protocol, isolating the difference (data split, candidate set, preprocessing). Then run the width sweep 64–512 only inside whichever protocol reproduces, and report the width trend under both.\"\n\nHmm, \"run the width sweep 64–512 only inside whichever protocol reproduces, and report the width trend under both.\" This is a bit ambiguous. I interpret: Once you know which protocol reproduces (i.e., which is the \"correct\" protocol), run the width sweep inside that protocol. Report the width trend under both protocols for completeness.\n\nNow for the plan, I need to understand:\n- The width sweep: hidden=300, emb_d=300 is the default. Width sweep 64-512 means varying the hidden dimension (and emb_d) from 64 to 512.\n\nActually, \"width\" of a neural network. The GISMo's ISD is an MLP with hidden dim 300 (hidden=300). The ingredient encoder GIN layers also have hidden dim. The width sweep 64-512 varies this.\n\nNow let me understand the compute requirements:\n- GISMo training: The model has 6,653 ingredients in the graph. Training is on ~49,044 substitution tuples with negative sampling (nr=400 negatives per positive). GIN layers = 2, hidden=300. This is a small model.\n- Training time: The paper says \"Results are averaged over 5 runs\". The compute is small — probably a few hours on a single GPU, or even CPU. Actually GISMo is quite lightweig