    "content": "Found 100 matches (more matches available)\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bd98e8e00192YR4sclD16to6:\n  Line 126: A GISMo Implementation Details\n\n  Line 189: Moreover, obtaining high-quality ingredient substitutions is not sufficient to personalize recipes, since substituted ingredients oftentimes require adapting the cooking instructions. For example, when baking a cake, we may want to use banana instead of egg. However, the way to process eggs – whisk eggs – is different from the way to process bananas – mash bananas. Therefore, learning-based approaches have been further extended in [33] to not only propose ingredient substitutions but also alter the cooking instructions accordingly. This has been achieved by introducing a fully supervised pipeline that assumes access to pairs of original and personalized recipes, which are rather rare to find at scale and which are unfortunately not made publicly available.\n\n  Line 199: The resulting updated set of ingredients is used along the input image to generate the edited recipe, leveraging the compositionality captured by language models to operate outside of training distribution at inference time as opposed to requiring full supervision to generate personalized recipes. We further improve this pipeline by taking advantage of a vision transformer (ViT) [15] backbone to predict the ingredients in the input image. Figure 1 depicts the proposed personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\n\n  Line 247: Evaluation metrics. We follow the literature on recommender systems and link predictors on graphs [61, 2, 17] and evaluate the ingredient substitution task with Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) and Hit@kk, k∈{1,3,10}k\\in\\{1,3,10\\}. Both MRR and Hit@kk rely on ranking the ingredients in 𝒟i​n​g∖{s}{\\mathcal{D}_{ing}}\\setminus\\{s\\} for each recipe and source ingredient. The MRR computes the multiplicative inverse of the rank of the correct substitution answer, as determined by yy. Hit@kk measures the proportion of correct substitutions that rank among the top kk answers. However, since there are cases for which different substitutions (target ingredients) might be proposed for the same source ingredient within a recipe, we avoid penalizing for such cases through their ranking by accepting all valid target ingredients as correct answers at evaluation time.\n\n  Line 293: We train all three modules of GISMo end-to-end using mini-batch stochastic gradient descent [5]. At each training iteration, the model takes in a batch of substitution tuples and recipes. In practice, as we only have positive substitution samples available in our data, we generate negative samples for each positive sample following [2, 61], i.e. for each positive sample, we obtain a subset of negative samples of cardinality ζ\\zeta by replacing yy in the valid substitution tuples with random ingredients from 𝒟i​n​g{\\mathcal{D}_{ing}}{}, one at a time.\n\n  Line 300: To enable recipe personalization, we integrate GISMo in the inverse cooking pipeline described in [49] and introduce the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. Figure 3 illustrates the full pipeline at inference time. First, an image is fed to the image encoder and an image embedding is obtained. The image embedding is then used to predict a set of ingredients. GISMo module acts on the predicted ingredient set and substitutes one selected ingredient specified by the user. Next, the ingredient encoder of inverse cooking takes as an input the modified ingredient set and produces ingredient embeddings. Finally, both the ingredient embeddings of the modified ingredient set as well as the image embedding are fed into a recipe decoder that outputs a generated and personalized recipe. Important modifications w.r.t. [49] include the image encoder architecture, where we swap ResNet [24] for Vision Transformers (ViT) [15]. We further extend the ViT architecture by concatenating features corresponding to odd layers’ outputs in the classification token as it proved to perform slightly better in our experiments than the vanilla ViT architecture. We refer to this modified ViT architecture as ViT Multi Layer Sequence, ViT MLS. Moreover, in contrast to [49] and as suggested in [46] we train our image encoder using the binary cross-entropy loss. At training time, we remove the GISMo module and train the pipeline following the inverse cooking approach. Thus, at inference time, the personalized inverse cooking pipeline operates out of training distribution.\n\n  Line 316: Ingredient vocabulary. Since FlavorGraph contains a subset of ingredients from Recipe1M, we process the ingredients in Recipe1M and Recipe1MSubs such that they match those in FlavorGraph, e.g. by merging singulars and plurals, different spellings of the same ingredient, or very infrequent ingredients with their closest match. As a result, our ingredient vocabulary contains 6,6536,\\!653 ingredients. Note that the original inverse cooking pipeline [49] uses a pre-processed vocabulary of 1,4881,\\!488 ingredients, which is more than 4×4\\times smaller than the FlavorGraph-based one.\n\n  Line 324: Personalized inverse cooking pipeline details. We train our pipeline following [49]. We start by training the image-to-ingredient prediction module, and then train the recipe generator using teacher forcing both from the ground truth ingredients and recipe. We add weight decay in the recipe generation training stage, as we found it helped mitigate the overfitting caused by the increased number of ingredients in our vocabulary. The weight decay was set to 10−510^{-5} after a sweep among [0.0,10−6,10−5,10−4,10−3][0.0,10^{-6},10^{-5},10^{-4},10^{-3}]. Note that GISMo is trained on its own and only used at inference time within the pipeline.\n\n  Line 415: Frequency (Freq): This baseline counts the number of times each v∈𝒟i​n​gv\\in{\\mathcal{D}_{ing}} is suggested as a target ingredient in τt​r​a​i​n\\tau_{train}, and uses these frequencies to rank ingredient substitutions. Lookup table + Frequency (LT+freq): This baseline combines the LT and Freq ranking approaches and computes how often a target ingredient appears for each source ingredient in τt​r​a​i​n\\tau_{train}. Those frequencies are then used to rank ingredient substitutions for each source ingredient. Food2Vec, FoodBERT, and R-FoodBERT: Those are language model-based approaches introduced in [45]. Food2Vec and FoodBERT finetune respectively word2vec [38] and BERT [13] models on cooking instructions of the Recipe1M dataset, and then use the updated word embeddings to suggest ingredients substitutions. The assumption is that ingredients that are close to each other in the embedding space are good candidate substitutions for each other. R-FoodBERT further finetunes the FoodBERT model on user comments crawled from the Recipe1M recipe websites, which contain substitution information. Metapath2vec [43]: This baseline implicitly learns structural node embeddings by leveraging the metapath2vec [14], an extension of node2vec [21] that operates on graphs with different node types. The approach enhances the node embedding learning of metapath2vec by including chemical property information.\n\n  Line 513: As it can be seen, the best performing version of GISMo has the three modules of IE\\mathrm{IE}, CE\\mathrm{CE}, and ISD\\mathrm{ISD}, suggesting that both contextual recipe information, as well as ingredient relations, are important for the ingredient substitution task. The ISD\\mathrm{ISD}-based model, which takes as input the ingredient features directly and does not leverage context nor the graph structure, achieves the lowest performance in terms of MRR. When adding context information to ISD\\mathrm{ISD}, improvements are dependent on the context used, with recipe titles appearing to be beneficial (CE\\mathrm{CE}t + ISD\\mathrm{ISD}). When considering the graph structure (IE\\mathrm{IE} + ISD\\mathrm{ISD}), we also notice improvements, which are however below those achieved when leveraging context and graph structure at the same time. When considering both the graph structure and contextual information (IE\\mathrm{IE} + CE\\mathrm{CE} + ISD\\mathrm{ISD}), the kind of contextual information used becomes less important, with all variants performing similarly. However, it is worth noting that IE\\mathrm{IE} + CE\\mathrm{CE}I + ISD\\mathrm{ISD} requires less computation at inference time since it does not need any extra module to embed the recipe title.\n\n  Line 580: Here, we test the effect of ingredient feature initialization on the overall model performance. Since the FlavorGraph does not come with ingredient features, GISMo uses a randomly initialized embedding layer which is updated during training. In this ablation, we leverage pretrained ingredient features learned by language models or through node structural encodings instead. In particular, we consider metapath2vec augmented with chemical structures [43], random walks (node2vec) [21], as well as FoodBERT ingredient features [45]. Additionally, we take the pretrained ingredient features which best perform OOD (FoodBERT) and enable finetuning of those features at training time 22\n\n  Line 640: In this experiment, we test if we can swap the source and target ingredients during training and maintain or improve the system’s performance. To this end, at training time, we introduce a data augmentation layer that swaps the source and target ingredients randomly with a probability of 0.50.5. The validation set MRR for this experiment reaches 30.61±0.1030.61\\pm 0.10, while the original GISMo model obtained 31.25±0.1231.25\\pm 0.12, thus we observe a decrease of 0.640.64 on average. This suggests that the directionality of the ingredients in the substitution tuple provides an important signal. This drop can be further explained by looking at examples, e.g. when cooking a cake one might substitute sugar for applesauce to make the cake healthier; however, it would be unlikely to substitute applesauce for sugar. In general, we observe that users tend to substitute rare, allergen, expensive, or unhealthy ingredients for their frequent, non-allergen, cheaper, or healthier versions.\n\n  Line 889: Multimedia, pages 1771–1779, 2017.\n\n  Line 1113: IEEE Transactions on Multimedia, 20(10):2836–2848, 2018.\n\n  Line 1136: Multimedia, pages 2188–2190, 2019.\n\n  Line 1323: Multimedia, MM ’20, page 4244–4252, New York, NY, USA, 2020. Association\n\n  Line 1481: In Proceedings of the ACM multimedia 2009 workshop on Multimedia\n\n  Line 1582: Rex Ying, Ruining He, Kaifeng Chen, Pong Eksombatchai, William L Hamilton, and\n\n  Line 1638: Appendix A GISMo Implementation Details\n\n  Line 1641: We implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 10001000. We tuned the learning rate l​rlr using the set {0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005}\\{0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005\\}, and both the number of features FF and ingredient embedding dimension dd using the set {200,300,400,500,600}\\{200,300,400,500,600\\}.\n\n  Line 1644: Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE\\mathrm{IE}) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substitution decoder (ISD\\mathrm{ISD}) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F=300F=300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to d=300d=300. The model is trained with a learning rate l​r=0.00005lr=0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.00010.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.250.25.\n\n  Line 1660: Finally, we look at some failure modes of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline when used in combination with GISMo, pointing to potential areas of improvement. Figure 11 shows how the creativity of GISMo to come up with ingredient substitutions might be negatively impacted by incomplete, wrong, or near duplicate ingredient predictions of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. Recall that, in this case, the ingredients predicted by the inverse cooking pipeline constitute the context of GISMo, therefore impacting the kind of substitutions that GISMo can propose. Improving the ingredient prediction step in the personalized inverse cooking pipeline holds the promise to improve GISMo’s suggestions as a result. Moreover, Figure 12 shows the phenomenon of recipe collapse that sometimes occurs when performing (even minor) substitutions with the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. However, as shown in Figure 13, this phenomenon also appears in the inverse cooking pipeline which does not consider any substitution, pointing to potential areas of improvement of the recipe generator module of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\n\n  Line 1672: Figure 8:  Comparing the performance of GISMo against the LT-freq approach by showing the top-5 suggestions for each approach on examples where the ingredient to substitute (jam or banana cream instant pudding) does not appear in the training set (out of distribution). GISMo is able to provide creative substitutions for ingredients never seen at the training time, whereas the LT-freq approach will fall in a default mode (returning ingredients by alphabetical order). Note that approximately half of the ingredients in the ingredients vocabulary never appear in the training set, so this generalization to unseen ingredients is very important.\n\n  Line 1720:     Our team has already identified the following issues. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bdd0fcc001k8NXbRJp1d5dVO:\n  Line 19: Most existing approaches to ingredient substitution are based on information extracted from textual recipes, including simple statistics – *e.g*. Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency [shidochi2009finding](#bib.bib53); [boscarino2014automatic](#bib.bib3); [yamanishi2015alternative](#bib.bib60) –, and ingredient co-occurrences [achananuparp2016extracting](#bib.bib1); [morales2021word](#bib.bib39); [lawo2020veganaizer](#bib.bib31); [kazama2018neural](#bib.bib29). Only recently, researchers have started to explore advances in natural language processing (NLP) – *e.g*. word2vec [word2vec](#bib.bib38), BERT [devlin2018bert](#bib.bib13), and R-BERT [wu2019enriching](#bib.bib58) – to obtain ingredient embeddings [foodbert](#bib.bib45); [shirai2020identifying](#bib.bib54), showing the benefits of representation learning to improve the task of ingredient substitution. This has been further emphasized with the development of node embedding methods to learn ingredient substitutions from the recently introduced large-scale FlavorGraph of ingredient relations and flavor molecules [park2021flavorgraph](#bib.bib43). However, all the above-mentioned methods assume that ingredients have universal substitutions that are suitable in the context of any recipe. Moreover, obtaining high-quality ingredient substitutions is not sufficient to personalize recipes, since substituted ingredients oftentimes require adapting the cooking instructions. For example, when baking a cake, we may want to use *banana* instead of *egg*. However, the way to process eggs – *whisk eggs* – is different from the way to process bananas – *mash bananas*. Therefore, learning-based approaches have been further extended in [li2021share](#bib.bib33) to not only propose ingredient substitutions but also alter the cooking instructions accordingly. This has been achieved by introducing a fully supervised pipeline that assumes access to pairs of original and personalized recipes, which are rather rare to find at sc...\n  Line 25: Finally, we plug GISMo into the inverse cooking system from [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49) to enable recipe editing. Inverse cooking first predicts the set of ingredients in an image and then generates a cooking recipe from the predicted ingredients and the input image. We modify the pipeline such that predicted ingredients are fed to GISMo together with a mask indicating the ingredient to be substituted, as specified by the user. The resulting updated set of ingredients is used along the input image to generate the edited recipe, leveraging the compositionality captured by language models to operate outside of training distribution at inference time as opposed to requiring full supervision to generate personalized recipes. We further improve this pipeline by taking advantage of a vision transformer (ViT) [dosovitskiy2021an](#bib.bib15) backbone to predict the ingredients in the input image. Figure [1](#S1.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") depicts the proposed personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\n\n  Line 65: Evaluation metrics. We follow the literature on recommender systems and link predictors on graphs [pinsage](#bib.bib61); [transe](#bib.bib2); [simple-plus](#bib.bib17) and evaluate the ingredient substitution task with Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) and Hit@kk, k∈{1,3,10}k\\\\in\\\\{1,3,10\\\\}. Both MRR and Hit@kk rely on *ranking* the ingredients in 𝒟i​n​g∖{s}{\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{ing}}\\\\setminus\\\\{s\\\\} for each recipe and source ingredient. The MRR computes the multiplicative inverse of the rank of the correct substitution answer, as determined by yy. Hit@kk measures the proportion of correct substitutions that rank among the top kk answers. However, since there are cases for which different substitutions (target ingredients) might be proposed for the same source ingredient within a recipe, we avoid penalizing for such cases through their ranking by accepting all valid target ingredients as correct answers at evaluation time.\n\n  Line 89: We train all three modules of GISMo end-to-end using mini-batch stochastic gradient descent [Bottou98on-linelearning](#bib.bib5). At each training iteration, the model takes in a batch of substitution tuples and recipes. In practice, as we only have positive substitution samples available in our data, we generate negative samples for each positive sample following [transe](#bib.bib2); [pinsage](#bib.bib61), *i.e*. for each positive sample, we obtain a subset of negative samples of cardinality ζ\\\\zeta by replacing yy in the valid substitution tuples with random ingredients from 𝒟i​n​g{\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{ing}}{}, one at a time.\n\n  Line 93: To enable recipe personalization, we integrate GISMo in the inverse cooking pipeline described in [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49) and introduce the *personalized inverse cooking pipeline*. Figure [3](#S3.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 3 GISMo ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") illustrates the full pipeline at inference time. First, an image is fed to the image encoder and an image embedding is obtained. The image embedding is then used to predict a set of ingredients. GISMo module acts on the predicted ingredient set and substitutes one selected ingredient specified by the user. Next, the ingredient encoder of inverse cooking takes as an input the modified ingredient set and produces ingredient embeddings. Finally, both the ingredient embeddings of the modified ingredient set as well as the image embedding are fed into a recipe decoder that outputs a generated and personalized recipe. Important modifications w.r.t. [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49) include the image encoder architecture, where we swap ResNet [HeZRS15](#bib.bib24) for Vision Transformers (ViT) [dosovitskiy2021an](#bib.bib15). We further extend the ViT architecture by concatenating features corresponding to odd layers’ outputs in the classification token as it proved to perform slightly better in our experiments than the vanilla ViT architecture. We refer to this modified ViT architecture as ViT Multi Layer Sequence, ViT MLS. Moreover, in contrast to [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49) and as suggested in [Pineda-1904](#bib.bib46) we train our image encoder using the binary cross-entropy loss. At training time, we remove the GISMo module and train the pipeline following the inverse cooking approach. Thus, at inference time, the personalized inverse cooking pipeline operates out of training distribution.\n\n  Line 103: Ingredient vocabulary. Since FlavorGraph contains a subset of ingredients from Recipe1M, we process the ingredients in Recipe1M and Recipe1MSubs such that they match those in FlavorGraph, *e.g*. by merging singulars and plurals, different spellings of the same ingredient, or very infrequent ingredients with their closest match. As a result, our ingredient vocabulary contains 6,6536,\\\\!653 ingredients. Note that the original inverse cooking pipeline [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49) uses a pre-processed vocabulary of 1,4881,\\\\!488 ingredients, which is more than 4×4\\\\times smaller than the FlavorGraph-based one.\n\n  Line 107: Personalized inverse cooking pipeline details. We train our pipeline following [salvador2019inverse](#bib.bib49). We start by training the image-to-ingredient prediction module, and then train the recipe generator using teacher forcing both from the ground truth ingredients and recipe. We add weight decay in the recipe generation training stage, as we found it helped mitigate the overfitting caused by the increased number of ingredients in our vocabulary. The weight decay was set to 10−510^{-5} after a sweep among \\[0.0,10−6,10−5,10−4,10−3\\]\\[0.0,10^{-6},10^{-5},10^{-4},10^{-3}\\]. Note that GISMo is trained on its own and only used at inference time within the pipeline.\n\n  Line 233: Baselines. We consider the following statistical, language model-based, and graph-based baselines. Random: This baseline randomly ranks all possible ingredient substitutions given a source ingredient and a recipe. Mode: This baseline finds the ingredient that appears the most often as a target ingredient across all data samples in τt​r​a​i​n\\\\tau\\_{train}, and uses it as a proposed substitution in all cases. Lookup table (LT): For each source ingredient, this baseline assigns rank 1 to all target ingredients that appear together with said source ingredient in the substitution tuples of τt​r​a​i​n\\\\tau\\_{train}. Frequency (Freq): This baseline counts the number of times each v∈𝒟i​n​gv\\\\in{\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{ing}} is suggested as a target ingredient in τt​r​a​i​n\\\\tau\\_{train}, and uses these frequencies to rank ingredient substitutions. Lookup table + Frequency (LT+freq): This baseline combines the LT and Freq ranking approaches and computes how often a target ingredient appears for each source ingredient in τt​r​a​i​n\\\\tau\\_{train}. Those frequencies are then used to rank ingredient substitutions for each source ingredient. Food2Vec, FoodBERT, and R-FoodBERT: Those are language model-based approaches introduced in [foodbert](#bib.bib45). Food2Vec and FoodBERT finetune respectively word2vec [word2vec](#bib.bib38) and BERT [devlin2018bert](#bib.bib13) models on cooking instructions of the Recipe1M dataset, and then use the updated word embeddings to suggest ingredients substitutions. The assumption is that ingredients that are close to each other in the embedding space are good candidate substitutions for each other. R-FoodBERT further finetunes the FoodBERT model on user comments crawled from the Recipe1M recipe websites, which contain substitution information. Metapath2vec [park2021flavorgraph](#bib.bib43): This baseline implicitly learns structural node embeddings by leveraging the metapath2vec [metapath2vec](#bib.bib14), an extension of node2vec [grover2016node2vec](...\n  Line 341: As it can be seen, the best performing version of GISMo has the three modules of IE\\\\mathrm{IE}, CE\\\\mathrm{CE}, and ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}, suggesting that both contextual recipe information, as well as ingredient relations, are important for the ingredient substitution task. The ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}\\-based model, which takes as input the ingredient features directly and does not leverage context nor the graph structure, achieves the lowest performance in terms of MRR. When adding context information to ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}, improvements are dependent on the context used, with recipe titles appearing to be beneficial (CE\\\\mathrm{CE}t + ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}). When considering the graph structure (IE\\\\mathrm{IE} + ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}), we also notice improvements, which are however below those achieved when leveraging context and graph structure at the same time. When considering both the graph structure and contextual information (IE\\\\mathrm{IE} + CE\\\\mathrm{CE} + ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}), the kind of contextual information used becomes less important, with all variants performing similarly. However, it is worth noting that IE\\\\mathrm{IE} + CE\\\\mathrm{CE}I + ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD} requires less computation at inference time since it does not need any extra module to embed the recipe title.\n\n  Line 435: Exploring different ingredient features as input. Here, we test the effect of ingredient feature initialization on the overall model performance. Since the FlavorGraph does not come with ingredient features, GISMo uses a randomly initialized embedding layer which is updated during training. In this ablation, we leverage *pretrained* ingredient features learned by language models or through node structural encodings instead. In particular, we consider metapath2vec augmented with chemical structures [park2021flavorgraph](#bib.bib43), random walks (node2vec) [grover2016node2vec](#bib.bib21), as well as FoodBERT ingredient features [foodbert](#bib.bib45). Additionally, we take the pretrained ingredient features which best perform OOD (FoodBERT) and enable finetuning of those features at training time 22 2 We use L2 regularization on the learnable values to avoid the input features going too far from the FoodBERT embeddings. From the results, we observe that leveraging pretrained ingredient features leads to an overall MRR performance that is comparable to the one achieved by learnable randomly initialized embeddings. However, when it comes to OOD results, structural node embeddings appear to bring some performance improvements, with node2vec achieving better results than metapath2vec. Those improvements are even more remarkable when leveraging embeddings from FoodBERT. However, those trends are not preserved ID. Finally, when enabling gradient propagation through FoodBERT, the benefits of generalizing OOD are reduced.\n\n  Line 499: Are substitutions directional? In this experiment, we test if we can swap the source and target ingredients during training and maintain or improve the system’s performance. To this end, at training time, we introduce a data augmentation layer that swaps the source and target ingredients randomly with a probability of 0.50.5. The validation set MRR for this experiment reaches 30.61±0.1030.61\\\\pm 0.10, while the original GISMo model obtained 31.25±0.1231.25\\\\pm 0.12, thus we observe a decrease of 0.640.64 on average. This suggests that the directionality of the ingredients in the substitution tuple provides an important signal. This drop can be further explained by looking at examples, *e.g*. when cooking a cake one might substitute sugar for applesauce to make the cake healthier; however, it would be unlikely to substitute applesauce for sugar. In general, we observe that users tend to substitute rare, allergen, expensive, or unhealthy ingredients for their frequent, non-allergen, cheaper, or healthier versions.\n\n  Line 650: -   (8) Jing-jing Chen, Chong-Wah Ngo, and Tat-Seng Chua. Cross-modal recipe retrieval with rich food attributes. In Proceedings of the 25th ACM international conference on Multimedia, pages 1771–1779, 2017.\n\n  Line 667: -   (25) Shota Horiguchi, Sosuke Amano, Makoto Ogawa, and K