00,400,500,600\\\\}.\n\n  Line 712: Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE\\\\mathrm{IE}) composed of two GIN [gin](#bib.bib59) layers, and an ingredient substitution decoder (ISD\\\\mathrm{ISD}) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F\\=300F=300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to d\\=300d=300. The model is trained with a learning rate l​r\\=0.00005lr=0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.00010.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.250.25.\n\n  Line 722: Finally, we look at some failure modes of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline when used in combination with GISMo, pointing to potential areas of improvement. Figure [11](#A2.F11 \"Figure 11 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") shows how the creativity of GISMo to come up with ingredient substitutions might be negatively impacted by incomplete, wrong, or near duplicate ingredient predictions of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. Recall that, in this case, the ingredients predicted by the inverse cooking pipeline constitute the context of GISMo, therefore impacting the kind of substitutions that GISMo can propose. Improving the ingredient prediction step in the personalized inverse cooking pipeline holds the promise to improve GISMo’s suggestions as a result. Moreover, Figure [12](#A2.F12 \"Figure 12 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") shows the phenomenon of recipe collapse that sometimes occurs when performing (even minor) substitutions with the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. However, as shown in Figure [13](#A2.F13 \"Figure 13 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\"), this phenomenon also appears in the inverse cooking pipeline which does not consider any substitution, pointing to potential areas of improvement of the recipe generator module of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\n\n  Line 738: Figure 8: Comparing the performance of GISMo against the LT-freq approach by showing the top-5 suggestions for each approach on examples where the ingredient to substitute (*jam* or *banana cream instant pudding*) does not appear in the training set (out of distribution). GISMo is able to provide creative substitutions for ingredients never seen at the training time, whereas the LT-freq approach will fall in a default mode (returning ingredients by alphabetical order). Note that approximately half of the ingredients in the ingredients vocabulary never appear in the training set, so this generalization to unseen ingredients is very important.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b9cece2001keapzhlspfiTgM:\n  Line 44: 16.  [D Reproducibility and Implementation Details](#A4 \"In Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")\n\n  Line 45:     1.  [D.1 Split hygiene: what is disjoint, and what is not](#A4.SS1 \"In Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")\n\n  Line 58: Neurosymbolic systems rely on mathematical solvers to guarantee reasoning correctness, yet solvers are fundamentally blind to whether a formal translation maintains strict reference-equivalence to a designated formalization. We formalize this vulnerability as Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness (VPU): a failure mode where an incorrect encoding executes successfully and matches the expected verdict. We theoretically prove that structural, verdict-only verification heuristics are mathematically bounded to chance-level detection on these deceptively valid traces. To resolve this, we introduce Generative Verification (GenV), which distills an offline Z3-equivalence oracle into a reference-free, continuous reference-equivalence score by repurposing the language model’s native vocabulary space. Mechanistic analysis via decision-projected logit lenses and sparse autoencoders shows this generative readout natively extracts precise spatial error coordinates without explicit localization training. Empirically, our oracle-mined verifier (GenV+HN) achieves 0.961 AUROC in reference-equivalence verification, generalizes zero-shot across unseen translators and divergent formal styles, and yields an 11.3-point downstream accuracy gain in agentic test-time compute allocation.\n\n  Line 72: Our central design choice separates strict oracle supervision from test-time deployment. Bidirectional Z3 checks generate decisive reference-equivalence labels. At deployment, where the designated reference is absent, GenV distills this offline signal into a functional, reference-free score. GenV repurposes the model’s standard token-prediction capabilities to produce a holistic Yes/No judgment, using normalized token probability as a continuous equivalence score instead of appending specialized classification layers or aggregating local step scores. Concentrating this supervision on oracle-certified VPUs through targeted mining yields the deployed GenV+HN verifier.\n\n  Line 82:     Reference-free generative verification: We distill offline bidirectional-equivalence labels into a continuous next-token score requiring only the source problem and candidate encoding at inference time.\n\n  Line 90:     Diagnostic representation analysis: We show that error-position and VPU information can be recovered from the verifier’s hidden states using prefix scores, gradient-based attribution, and sparse-feature probes, while treating these analyses as diagnostic rather than causal.\n\n  Line 99: Learned verification and test-time compute. While generative verifiers ([Zhang et al., 2025a](#bib.bib4)) and reward models ([Lightman et al., 2024](#bib.bib3)) improve test-time compute allocation ([Kang et al., 2025](#bib.bib39)), GenV+HN uniquely distills an exact offline SMT equivalence oracle. Unlike standard verifiers, GenV learns a diagnostic, reference-free generative readout directly from oracle labels, optimized via programmatic hard negatives ([Bengio et al., 2009](#bib.bib8)) to guide selective allocation ([Chen et al., 2024](#bib.bib26)).\n\n  Line 135: Proposition [1](#Thmproposition1 \"Proposition 1 (Binary verdict-only indistinguishability). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") isolates a narrow information limit: the binary solver verdict is insufficient on verdict-matched pairs. It does not establish an impossibility result for verifiers that inspect xx, ss, solver models, proof traces, or learned hidden representations. At deployment, the designated reference s⋆s^{\\\\star} is unavailable. The system must therefore learn a continuous, reference-free scoring function:\n\n  Line 149: Verification via Next Token Prediction. Standard reward modeling often structurally appends a randomly initialized classification head, a practice that can disrupt pre-trained representations and introduce optimization instability. Instead, we adopt an architecture-agnostic, functional approach: we repurpose the frozen, pre-trained vocabulary head to act as a generative classifier. By formatting the source xx via fixed system prompt, we constrain the language model to act as a referee which outputs a single token judgment (e.g., \"Yes\" for faithful, \"No\" for unfaithful; see Appendix  [D](#A4 \"Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\"))\n\n  Line 159: Where, ϵ\\=10−9\\\\epsilon=10^{-9} is a stabilization constant. By operating directly on the output logits rather than relying on discrete sampling or majority voting, the verifier preserves fine-grained confidence rankings without introducing inference-time variance.\n\n  Line 187: Evaluation Protocol. We quantify detection performance using the AUROC. To evaluate practical downstream utility, we measure end-to-end answer accuracy across two distinct deployment paradigms: (1) adaptive Proof of Thought ([Ganguly et al., 2024](#bib.bib32); [Ganguly et al., 2025](#bib.bib33); [Anonymous, 2026](#bib.bib18)), an agentic framework (explained in Figure [10](#A3.F10 \"Figure 10 ‣ Appendix C Extended Empirical Evaluations and Robustness ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")) where the verifier actively allocates test-time compute, and (2) static Best-of-N selection, a non-agentic baseline where the verifier is constrained to scoring a fixed set of pre-computed candidate encodings.\n\n  Line 191: We structure our empirical analysis around four fundamental research questions, systematically validating the generative verification framework from foundational reference-equivalence verification through to test-time agentic utility.\n\n  Line 1027: Table 7: Non-agentic one-shot re-ranking over K=5 independent single-shot encodings (8 backends ×\\\\times 9 datasets = 7,200 pools). The final column isolates GenV+HN’s paired gain over the 1-shot and Vote@3 baselines.\n\n  Line 1177: Equation [9](#S7.E9 \"In 7 Inside the Verifier ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") is a first-order attribution of the decision margin to an intermediate activation, in the gradient-times-activation family ([Shrikumar et al., 2017](#bib.bib30)), and we call it a decision-projected gradient lens to distinguish it from the logit lens, which applies the unembedding to a hidden state without any gradient ([nostalgebraist, 2020](#bib.bib28)). Related work on Jacobian lenses ([Gurnee et al., 2026](#bib.bib29)) likewise projects intermediate states to read internal structure; we use the projection only as a diagnostic measure and make no claim about causal mechanism or a localized workspace.\n\n  Line 1189: A linear probe on the sparse features recovers 0.960 AUROC for GenV+HN. Probes on the PRM and ORM hidden states also recover substantially more VPU information than their deployed output scores expose. This suggests that differences among the models arise partly from how available information is read out. Together, the gradient and SAE analyses show that VPU and error-position information are diagnostically recoverable from intermediate representations. Because these analyses do not intervene on the identified activations, they do not establish a causal verification mechanism.\n\n  Line 1193: Neurosymbolic systems often rely on solver validity as a proxy for reasoning correctness, yet this leaves them blind to flawed translations that deceptively yield the expected verdict. We formalize this vulnerability as Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness (VPU), proving that structural execution checks fundamentally fail to detect these mismatches. GenV+HN resolves this by distilling offline Z3-equivalence into a reference-free generative readout. Diagnostic analysis via gradient lenses and sparse autoencoders demonstrates that this readout naturally exposes an internal signal for error localization without explicit training, overcoming the chance-level bounds of verdict-only heuristics. Empirically, GenV+HN sustains ∼0.96{\\\\sim}0.96 AUROC across unseen translators and drives substantial accuracy gains in agentic test-time compute allocation. While bounded by formal-style shifts and the inherent ambiguity of human intent, GenV+HN provides a robust, deployable safeguard for reference-equivalence exactly where offline solver guarantees end.\n\n  Line 1207: Computational Budget. All verifiers share a frozen 27B parameter instruction-tuned backbone. Training the GenV models via Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) required approximately 12 GPU hours on a single NVIDIA H100-80GB GPU. Evaluation and test-time agentic loop inference were conducted using standard half-precision (bf16) deployments on identical hardware.\n\n  Line 1218: -   Beer et al. (2001) I. Beer, S. Ben-David, C. Eisner, and Y. Rodeh Efficient detection of vacuity in temporal model checking. Formal Methods in System Design 18 (2), pp. 141–163. External Links: [Document](https://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A%3A1008779610539), [Link](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023/A:1008779610539.pdf) Cited by: [§2](#S2.p2.1 \"2 Related Work ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\").\n\n  Line 1227: -   Hu et al. (2022) E. J. Hu, Y. Shen, P. Wallis, Z. Allen-Zhu, Y. Li, S. Wang, L. Wang, and W. Chen LoRA: low-rank adaptation of large language models. In International Conference on Learning Representations, External Links: [Link](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=nZeVKeeFYf9) Cited by: [Appendix D](#A4.p6.1 \"Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\").\n\n  Line 1261: The *logit lens* projects an intermediate hidden state through the unembedding ([nostalgebraist, 2020](#bib.bib28)). Jacobian lenses additionally propagate a residual through an input-output Jacobian ([Gurnee et al., 2026](#bib.bib29)). We utilize a context-specific specialization: with a Yes-versus-No margin gg, the score ⟨∂g/∂h,h⟩\\\\langle\\\\partial g/\\\\partial h,h\\\\rangle represents the gradient times activation projected onto the model’s own decision. This acts as a local first-order attribution under a linear approximation ([Shrikumar et al., 2017](#bib.bib30)).\n\n  Line 1373: Setup. For GenV+HN, the rationale control, PRM, and ORM, we hook the residual stream at layer 48 and collect activations at every token position over the 950-row combined benchmark (∼3.5×105\\\\sim\\\\!3.5\\\\times 10^{5} vectors). We train a TopK SAE (width 16384, K\\=50K=50 active features per token) purely by reconstruction, completely devoid of reference-equivalence supervision.\n\n  Line 1560: Table 14: Non-agentic one-shot re-ranking over K\\=5K{=}5 independent single-shot encodings (88 backends ×\\\\times 99 datasets \\=7,200\\=7{,}200 pools; identical pool per rule): single-shot baseline (1-shot), self-consistency (Vote@33), GenV+HN reference-equivalence-weighted best-of-55 (++GenV), and pool oracle. Final column: GenV+HN’s paired gain over 1-shot / over Vote@33 (exact McNemar, ∗\\=p<0.05{}^{\\*}{=}p{<}0.05). The equal K\\=5K{=}5\\-budget comparison (−0.7\\-0.7 vs vote@55) is the no-escalation ablation of Table [6](#S6.T6 \"Table 6 ‣ 6 Results and Analysis ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") it isolates the gate, not the re-ranker, as the verifier’s contribution.\n\n  Line 1682: Table 15: One-shot reranking, four-backend/six-dataset detail (K\\=5K{=}5 encodings/problem, 100100 problems ×\\\\times six datasets, identical pool per rule): first candidate, self-consistency vote, two scaled readouts of the same checkpoint ([Kwok et al., 2026](#bib.bib5)) (granularity-2020 expectation G20, and holistic-plus-criteria ensemble crit), GenV+HN’s binary readout, and pool oracle. Last two columns: GenV+HN’s paired gain over vote / over first (exact paired McNemar, ∗\\=p<0.05{}^{\\*}{=}p{<}0.05). Neither scaled readout beats GenV+HN (G20 p\\=0.26p{=}0.26, ensemble p\\=0.12p{=}0.12 pooled).\n\n  Line 1784: And it cannot be reached for a VPU row. That is forced by the mining predicate rather than lucky. A retained mutant must satisfy v⁡(s′)\\=v⁡(s⋆)v(s^{\\\\prime}){=}v(s^{\\\\star}), the first factor of Equation [2](#S3.E2 \"In Definition 1 (Reference-relative Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\"), so an unsatisfiable reference admits only unsatisfiable mutants; those test as equivalent under the bidirectional check and are then rejected by the second factor 1−Eq⁡(s′,s⋆)1-\\\\mathrm{Eq}(s^{\\\\prime},s^{\\\\star}). An unsatisfiable reference can therefore contribute reference-equivalent rows but never a VPU row, which is what the census shows. The degeneracy