against the full prompt on the same resamples.\n\n  Line 1889: ## Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details\n\n  Line 1891: Base model and readout. All verifiers share one frozen base, an open 2727B instruction-tuned language model (Qwen3.6-27B), served in bf16. The generative score of Equation [4](#S4.E4 \"In 4 Generative verification ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") reads the next-token distribution at the position after the referee prompt and renormalizes over the Yes and No token-id sets. Those sets are the first token ids returned by the tokenizer for the surface strings {\\\\{“Yes”, “ Yes”, “yes”, “ yes”}\\\\} and {\\\\{“No”, “ No”, “no”, “ no”}\\\\} respectively, deduplicated; the leading-space and lowercase variants matter because the chat template can place the answer after a space. The stabilizer is ε\\=10−9\\\\varepsilon=10^{-9}. No sampling is used at scoring time; the readout is a single deterministic forward pass.\n\n  Line 1927: Zero-shot judge baseline. The judge baseline is a strong proprietary instruction-tuned chat model (Claude Opus 4.8, served via Amazon Bedrock) prompted zero-shot with the same problem-and-encoding presentation as the referee prompt. We elicit three readouts on the same 462462 rows: (i) *graded*, one deterministic call asking for an integer 00–100100 faithfulness probability, used as the continuous score (0.8380.838, the judge’s strongest readout on this split and the headline comparison of Section [6](#S6 \"6 Results and Analysis ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")); (ii) *single-token*, one deterministic one-word Yes/No call scored 1/01/0 (0.7180.718; paired Δ\\\\Delta vs the verifier +0.24{+}0.24, CI \\[+0.08,+0.40\\]\\[{+}0.08,{+}0.40\\], ℙ⁡(Δ\\>0)\\=1.00\\\\mathbb{P}(\\\\Delta{>}0){=}1.00); and (iii) *sampled Yes-fraction*, K\\=5K{=}5 completions at the default sampling temperature scored as the fraction answering Yes (0.700.70–0.710.71 across runs). The judge sees neither the gold reference nor the solver verdict, matching the trained verifier’s deployment information set. It agrees with the Z3 oracle on roughly two thirds of labeled cases. This target mismatch motivates training directly on oracle labels, but it is not a ceiling on either model’s performance. Because the judge is a proprietary hosted model that may be updated, this comparison is a point-in-time measurement; we pin the exact model identifier and prompt in our released artifacts for re-runs against the same snapshot.\n\n  Line 1929: Oracle: Z3 version, limits, and equivalence procedure. Labels come from Z3 4.16.04.16.0. Each call runs under a wall-clock timeout of 50005000 ms *and* a deterministic resource limit (rlimit \\=2×107\\=2\\\\times 10^{7}); the resource limit, not the wall clock, governs reproducibility because it is machine-independent. Two encodings that share a signature are tested for logical equivalence by parsing both in a *single shared* Z3 context so that identically-named declarations resolve to the same symbol, forming A\\=⋀(asserts in ​s′)A=\\\\bigwedge(\\\\text{asserts in }s^{\\\\prime}) and B\\=⋀(asserts in ​s⋆)B=\\\\bigwedge(\\\\text{asserts in }s^{\\\\star}), and checking that both A∧¬BA\\\\wedge\\\\neg B and B∧¬AB\\\\wedge\\\\neg A are unsat; s′≡s⋆s^{\\\\prime}\\\\equiv s^{\\\\star} iff both are unsat. Model theoretic equivalence identifies all unsatisfiable formulas, since both denote the empty model set. This is immaterial here: every one of the 950 benchmark encodings and all 950 gold references are satisfiable, as are all but 21 of the public-transfer encodings, and those 21 are reference-equivalent rows (an unsatisfiable gold cannot yield a VPU, because the mining filter requires v⁡(s′)\\=v⁡(s⋆)v(s^{\\\\prime})=v(s^{\\\\star}) and two unsatisfiable programs are equivalent under this test, so the candidate is rejected). Where inherently unsatisfiable specifications are in scope, the check would