,4291{,}429 VPU, a 57:4357{:}43 class balance. The two-class reward-model heads add a single linear layer (22 logits over the 51205120\\-dim hidden state, ∼10​K\\\\sim 10K parameters) on top of the *same* adapter set, ∼200​M\\\\sim 200M trainable parameters; the generative readout adds no parameters at all, so the readout comparison is not confounded by capacity.\n\n  Line 1935: Reproducibility statement. On acceptance we release all code, data manifests, frozen test checksums, and per-run leaderboards: the eval harness, sibling-margin probe, hard-negative miner, fresh-problem authoring script, and paired comparison. Labels are Z3-equivalence to the gold reference, hence deterministically reproducible; the base model, Z3 version and limits, equivalence procedure, and the verbatim referee and judge prompts are all above. GenV and GenV+HN are each retrained at seeds 0,1,2 (Table [17](#A4.T17 \"Table 17 ‣ Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")); most secondary ablations are single runs unless marked otherwise. Problem-clustered bootstrap intervals quantify evaluation-set uncertainty and should not be read as training-seed intervals.\n\n  Line 1951: Case studies below, works through four VPU pairs one assertion at a time. Every pair starts from a gold encoding s⋆s^{\\\\star} that the Z3 equivalence oracle of Equation [1](#S3.E1 \"In 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") labels faithful. We then change exactly one operator to obtain a candidate s−s^{-} that is still syntactically correct, still returns sat, and is no longer equivalent to the gold, which is the VPU condition of Definition [1](#Thmdefinition1 \"Definition 1 (Reference-relative Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness). ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\"). Both programs therefore carry the same solver certificate, so the verdict on its own cannot separate them. The four samples span three edit families and put the defect at different depths: last of ten in A, ninth of ten in B, eighth of ten in C, and first of seven in D. Each panel scores the pair with GenV+HN, a process reward model (PRM), and an outcome reward model (ORM) on one shared scale from 0 to 1. GenV+HN scores each conjunct in its own forward pass, so the passes are independent. The PRM emits a score at every step token, and its segmentation adds two positions that are not assertions, the declaration block and the closing check-sat; these are hatched. The ORM emits one score for the whole program and has no step axis, so it is drawn as a single cell spanning the strip. Three results follow from reading the rows. First, the ORM cannot express the failure at all: on sample A it returns 0.21870.2187 for the correct program and 0.21870.2187 for the broken one. Second, taking the minimum over the step vector is the only aggregation that stays positive on all four pairs (mean Δ\\\\Delta 0.9430.943), because the mean dilutes one bad assertion across the whole program and the last step depends on where the defect happens to sit, inverting on B and C. Third, the argmin over assertions rec...\n  Line 1963: Our team has already identified [the following issues](https://github.com/arXiv/html_feedback/issues). We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14ba0ba42001Z7Zu5t2stUBgBf:\n  Line 44: 16.  [D Reproducibility and Implementation Details](#A4 \"In Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")\n\n  Line 45:     1.  [D.1 Split hygiene: what is disjoint, and what is not](#A4.SS1 \"In Appendix D Reproducibility and Implementation Details ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\")\n\n  Line 58: Neurosymbolic systems rely on mathematical solvers to guarantee reasoning correctness, yet solvers are fundamentally blind to whether a formal translation maintains strict reference-equivalence to a designated formalization. We formalize this vulnerability as Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness (VPU): a failure mode where an incorrect encoding executes successfully and matches the expected verdict. We theoretically prove that structural, verdict-only verification heuristics are mathematically bounded to chance-level detection on these deceptively valid traces. To resolve this, we introduce Generative Verification (GenV), which distills an offline Z3-equivalence oracle into a reference-free, continuous reference-equivalence score by repurposing the language model’s native vocabulary space. Mechanistic analysis via decision-projected logit lenses and sparse autoencoders shows this generative readout natively extracts precise spatial error coordinates without explicit localization training. Empirically, our oracle-mined verifier (GenV+HN) achieves 0.961 AUROC in reference-equivalence verification, generalizes zero-shot across unseen translators and divergent formal styles, and yields an 11.3-point downstream accuracy gain in agentic test-time compute allocation.\n\n  Line 72: Our central design choice separates strict oracle supervision from test-time deployment. Bidirectional Z3 checks generate decisive reference-equivalence labels. At deployment, where the designated reference is absent, GenV distills this offline signal into a functional, reference-free score. GenV repurposes the model’s standard token-prediction capabilities to produce a holistic Yes/No judgment, using normalized token probability as a continuous equivalence score instead of appending specialized classification layers or aggregating local step scores. Conc