    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_36307134883b110cb27175a118ffd64e\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Our approach improved Hit@1 accuracy on ingredient substitution tasks, from the baseline 34.53 ± 0.10% to 38.03 ± 0.28% on the original GISMo dataset, and from 40.24 ± 0.36% to 54.46 ± 0.29% on a refined version of the same dataset.\\nBy constructing a benchmark dataset, Recipe1MSubs, which includes ingredient substitution pairs extracted from user comments, GISMo significantly outperforms previous methods in ranking plausible ingredient substitutions. With a performance improvement of, at least, 14% in the top substitute ingredient prediction (Hit@1) over existing models [20].\\nThe original GISMo model was trained in this dataset using a methodology focused on ingredient context and co-occurrence as the benchmark of our study.\\n2.2. GISMo Benchmark\\nFrom these, a randomly selected dataset with 44,615 ingredient substitutions, divided into 31,063 for training, 6,831 for validation, and 6,721 for testing, was used to rerun GISMo and fine-tune LLMs as detailed below.\\nRunning the GISMo model five times with both the original and filtered datasets yielded\\nHit@1 accuracies of 34.53 ± 0.10% and 40.24 ± 0.36%, respectively. Integrating food categories (Appendix A) as additional node features into our computational model with the original dataset resulted in a Hit@1 value of 34.62% (Appendix D).\\nSubsequent experiments with fine-tuned LLMs on the original GISMo ingredient substitution\\ndataset yielded Hit@1 values of 20.09 ± 0.31% for DaVinci-002, 20.93 ± 0.29% for TinyLlama-1.1B, and 38.03 ± 0.28% for GPT-3.5-Turbo-1106. Using the filtered dataset in conjunction with these fine-tuned models resulted in improved Hit@1 values of 29.43 ± 0.30%, 34.53 ± 0.32%, and 54.46 ± 0.29%, respectively (Appendix D).\\nAppendix E for details).\\nTable 1 Performance of Fine-Tuned Models. Performance evaluation of fine-tuned models DaVinci-002, TinyLlama-1.1B, GPT-3.5-Turbo-1106, and GISMo, tested on both the original and filtered Recipe1MSubs datasets. The displayed Hit@1 accuracies are presented as mean ± standard deviation.\\nHowever, contrary to our expectations, this modification did not yield any improvements in the model's performance. This outcome may be attributed to several factors. Firstly, including this\\nRecipe1MSubs Dataset Fine Tuned Model Hit@1 (%)\\nFiltered GPT-3.5-Turbo-1106 54.46 ± 0.29\\nGISMo 40.24 ± 0.36\\nTinyLlama-1.1B 34.53 ± 0.32\\nTinyLlama-1.1B (Filtered Dataset) 35.07 34.22 34.46 34.53 34.37 34.53 ± 0.32\\nDaVinci-002 (Filtered Dataset) 28.97 29.59 29.61 29.27 29.70 29.43 ± 0.30\\nUnfiltered\\nGPT-3.5-Turbo-1106 38.08 38.25 37.96 38.28 37.59 38.03 ± 0.28\\nGISMo 34.55 34.42 34.68 34.54 34.45 34.53 ± 0.10\\nTinyLlama-1.1B 20.44 20.78 20.35 20.18 20.16 20.38 ± 0.25\\nRita L, Neumann NR, Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Veselkov D, Pratico D, et al. Alzheimer’s disease: using gene/protein network machine learning for molecule discovery in olive oil. Hum Genomics. 2023;17:57.\\n31. Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Shepherd M, Qureshi A, Veselkov D, Charkoftaki G, et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - geoscience-community-codes/GISMO: GISMO - a seismic data analysis toolbox for MATLAB · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\\n* Description: GISMO - a seismic data analysis toolbox for MATLAB - geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\\n* Stars: 125\\n* Forks: 48\\n* License: MIT license\\n* Default branch: master\\n* Created: 2015-10-15T21:09:20.000Z\\n* Commits: 652\\n1. Sign up for the Users Group\\n2. Download/Install GISMO\\n3. Explore the GISMO wiki...\\n4. ... especially the tutorials\\n5. Visit the GISMO website\\n6. Get help with GISMO\\n7. and please cite GISMO in your work\\n  ** Last updated: Glenn Thompson 2023/04/13\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/cliao5/GISMO\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - cliao5/GISMO · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Navigation Menu\\nToggle navigation\\nSign in\\nAppearance settings\\nSearch or jump to...\\nSearch code, repositories, users, issues, pull requests...\\nSign in\\nSign up\\nAppearance settings\\nResetting focus\\ncliao5 / GISMO Public\\n* Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\\n* Fork 0\\n* Star 1\\n* Code\\n* Issues\\n* Pull requests\\n* Actions\\n* Projects\\n* Security and quality\\n* Insights\\ncliao5/GISMO\\nmain\\nBranches Tags\\nGo to file\\nCode\\nOpen more actions menu\\nFolders and files\\n* Name | Name | Last commit message | Last commit date\\n* Latest commit\\nHistory\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - facebookresearch/gismo: companion code for \\\"Learning to substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\\" · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Evaluation with GISMo\\nGISMo model allows to provide substitution suggestion. For each of the validation inputs, this model will write potential substitutions. To evaluate the impact of substitutions generated by GISMo on the image-to-recipe pipeline, you can perform the following test.\\nFirst, measure the perplexity of recipes with no substitutions (this command sub-select the samples of the validate set that do have substitutions):\\npython eval.py task=im2recipe\\ndataset.eval_split=val_all\\nname=eval_im2recipe_vit16_multi_level_recipe_inference\\ndataset.ablation.with_substitutions=True\\neval_checkpoint_dir=<<PATH_2>>\\nThen, measure the perplexity of recipes with ground truth substitutions (on the same sub-set of the validation set):\\npython eval.py task=im2recipe\\ndataset.eval_split=val_all\\nname=eval_im2recipe_vit16_multi_level_recipe_inference\\ndataset.ablation.with_substitutions=True\\neval_checkpoint_dir=<<PATH_2>>\\nexperiments.im2recipe.eval_im2recipe_vit16_multi_level_recipe_inference.ingr_teachforce.test=use_substitutions\\nFinally, measure the perplexity of recipes with GISMO substitutions (on the same sub-set of the validation set):\\npython eval.py task=im2recipe\\ndataset.eval_split=val_all\\nname=eval_im2recipe_vit16_multi_level_recipe_inference\\nVisualisation with GISMo\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://chatpaper.com/chatpaper/paper/58943\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance phytochemical content and create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nOur approach improved Hit@1 accuracy on ingredient substitution tasks, from the baseline 34.53 plus-minus 0.10% to 38.03 plus-minus 0.28% on the original GISMo dataset, and from 40.24 plus-minus 0.36% to 54.46 plus-minus 0.29% on a refined version of the same dataset.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://gismo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference.html\",\n      \"title\": \"Reference — GISMO 0.4.3 documentation\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Parameters: | cluster_list (list of Cluster) – The clusters to merge into one cluster.focus (float) – Evaluation of the focus (similarity) between clusters.\\n* Returns: | The cluster merging the list.\\n* Return type: | Cluster\\n* gismo.clustering.\\nrec_clusterize (*cluster_list: list*, *resolution=0.7*) [source] ¶\\n> gismo.parameters.target_k = .2 # The toy dataset is very small, so we lower the auto_k parameter.\\n> gismo.get_documents_by_rank()\\n* rank (*query=''*, *y=True*, ***kwargs*) [source] ¶\\nRuns the DIteration using query as starting point. query can be evaluated on features (y=True) or documents (y=False).\\n> from gismo.datasets.reuters import get_reuters_news\\n> corpus = Corpus(get_reuters_news(), to_text=lambda e: e['content'])\\n> embedding = Embedding()\\n> embedding.fit_transform(corpus)\\n> gismo = Gismo(corpus, embedding)\\n> sentencer = Sentencizer(gismo)\\nIf not set, the auto_k() heuristic of the document-level Gismo will be used.s (int (optional)) – Number of sentences to return.\\nDatasets ¶\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nDEFAULT_FIELDS * = {'authors', 'title', 'type', 'venue', 'year'}* ¶\\nDefault fields to extract.\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nDTD_URL * = 'https://dblp.uni-trier.de/xml/dblp.dtd'* ¶\\nURL of the dtd file (required to correctly parse non-ASCII characters).\\n* *class*\\ngismo.datasets.dblp.\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nLIST_TYPE_FIELDS * = {'authors', 'urls'}* ¶\\nDBLP fields with possibly multiple entries.\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nURL * = 'https://dblp.uni-trier.de/xml/dblp.xml.gz'* ¶\\nURL of the full DBLP database.\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nelement_to_filesource (*elt*, *data_handler*, *index*, *fields*) [source] ¶\\nMust be writable.index – a list that contains the initial position of the data_handler for all previously processed elements.fields (set) – Set of fields to retrieve.\\n* Returns: | Always return True for compatibility with the xml parser.\\n* Return type: | bool\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nelement_to_source (*elt*, *source*, *fields*) [source] ¶\\nTest if elt is an article, converts it to dictionary and appends to source\\n* Parameters: | elt (Any) – a XML element.source (list) – the source in construction.fields (set) – Set of fields to retrieve.\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nfast_iter (*context*, *func*, *d=2*, ***kwargs*) [source] ¶\\n* gismo.datasets.dblp.\\nxml_element_to_dict (*elt*, *fields*) [source] ¶\\nConverts the xml element elt into a dict if it is a paper.\\n* Parameters: | elt (Any) – a XML element.fields (set) – Set of entries to retrieve.\\n* Returns: | Article dictionary if element contains the attributes of an article, None otherwise.\\n* Return type: | dict or None\\n* Returns: | entry – dict with keys set (C50test or c50train), author, id, and content\\n* Return type: | dict\\n* gismo.datasets.reuters.\\nget_reuters_news (*url='https://github.com/balouf/datasets/raw/main/C50.zip'*) [source] ¶\\nReturns a list of news from the Reuters C50 news datasets\\nAcknowledgments\\nDua, D. and Graff, C. (2019).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/52707-geoscience-community-codes-gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"geoscience-community-codes/GISMO - File Exchange - MATLAB Central\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-04-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\\nGISMO - a framework for scientific research in seismology/infrasound\\nhttps://github.com/geoscience-community-codes/GISMO\\nGlenn Thompson\\nGlenn Thompson\\nVersion 1.2.2.0 (24.5 MB)\\n6.6K Downloads\\n5.00/5 (6)\\n13 Apr 2023\\nDownload\\nShare 'geoscience-community-codes/GISMO'\\nOpen in File Exchange\\nOpen in MATLAB Online\\nClose\\nOpen in MATLAB Online\\nShare\\nFollow\\nProject website: https://geoscience-community-codes.github.io/GISMO GISMO is designed to allow easy retrieval of seismic waveform data, event catalogs and station metadata from a variety of data formats, databases and online data sources, eliminating a major barrier to rapid development of new seismic research programs and workflows, new seismic\\nmonitoring tools, etc.\\nThe foundation is a set of object oriented classes that describe different seismic data types and have built-in methods for loading from common formats and sources, and common visualization and processing tools.\\nGISMO is a community driven project that aims to encourage scientists to write code that builds on GISMO and then contributed code back to the project, so that GISMO can become ever more capable - and useful to more scientists.\\nAn aim is to make research more repeatable and lower the cost of research by providing a set of tools that allow scientists to get to the important part of research faster - doing the science, rather than the frustrations of how data is stored and how to get it in to MATLAB.\\nAdditional information can be found via the GISMO wiki: https://github.com/geoscience-community-codes/GISMO/wiki\\nGISMO (which includes The Waveform Suite) and related codes can also be discussed at: http://groups.google.com/group/gismotools\\nAs always, Comments are welcome, as are reviews and bug reports. Thanks!\\nCite As\\nGlenn Thompson (2026). geoscience-community-codes/GISMO (https://github.com/geoscience-community-codes/GISMO), GitHub. Retrieved September 4, 2026 .\\nAcknowledgements\\nInspired by: The Waveform Suite for MATLAB\\nCategories\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/slacgismo\",\n      \"title\": \"SLAC GISMo · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Organization: SLAC GISMo (@slacgismo)\\n* Page: GitHub organization profile\\n* URL: https://github.com/slacgismo\\n* Location: SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, Menlo Park, CA 94025\\n* Website: https://gismo.slac.stanford.edu/\\n* Top languages: Python, Jupyter Notebook, HTML\\nRepositories\\n* slacgismo/regrow\\n* slacgismo/ghrs-data: github-repository-stat data branch\\n* slacgismo/pv-validation-hub\\n* slacgismo/pv-validation-hub-client\\n* slacgismo/ascript: Advanced Smart Charting Infrastructure Planning Tool\\n* slacgismo/devine\\n* slacgismo/sg2t: SLAC GISMo Grid Tools\\n* slacgismo/recs_data: Residential Energy Consumption Survey data access\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/gismo/gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - gismo/gismo: G+Smo (pronounced gismo or gizmo) is a C++ library for isogeometric analysis (IGA). Geometry plus simulation modules aims at the seamless integration of Computer-aided Design (CAD) and Finite Element Analysis (FEA). · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"======================================================================\\n=====             Geometry plus Simulation modules               =====\\n=====                   https://github.com/gismo                 =====\\nTo enable e.g. gsSpectra and gsOpenCascade set the following option in CMake:\\n-D GISMO_OPTIONAL=\\\"gsSpectra;gsOpenCascade\\\"\\n======================================================================\\n=====             Geometry plus Simulation modules               =====\\n=====                   https://github.com/gismo                 =====\\nConfiguration Options\\nThe available options are displayed at CMake configuration. Short description and default setting follows:\\n======================================================================\\n=====             Geometry plus Simulation modules               =====\\n=====                   https://github.com/gismo                 =====\\nAvailable options include double, long double, float.\\n* GISMO_EXTRA_INSTANCE not set\\n======================================================================\\n=====             Geometry plus Simulation modules               =====\\n=====                   https://github.com/gismo                 =====\\nIf set to one or more of the options available for GISMO_COEFF_TYPE the G+Smo library is compiled with extra arithmetic types\\n======================================================================\\n=====             Geometry plus Simulation modules               =====\\n=====                   https://github.com/gismo                 =====\\nenabled.\\n* GISMO_WITH_XDEBUG OFF\\nIf set to ON additional debugging tools are enabled during compilation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/topics/gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"Topic: GISMo | SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* About\\n* Research\\n* Facilities & Centers\\n* Work with Us\\n* News & Events\\n  Search\\n1. Home\\n2. Explore SLAC topics\\n3. …\\n  Share Share Send\\n  SLAC topics\\nGISMo\\nGISMo works with universities, state agencies, utilities and industry to develop technology for the next-generation electric grid so it can seamlessly absorb power fluctuations and quickly respond to major storms and other disruptions.\\nBrowse tagged content\\n[GISMo lab's innovative plan to make electricity cheaper, greener and more reliable](  /news/2022-02-28-gismo-labs-innovative-plan-make-electricity-cheaper-greener-and-more-reliable\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_eb4182d76ffe1o5jpZBpXFC334\"\n}"
